A||||14||AkursedX||Brake Problem (88)||||1||
Z||000000||AkursedX||06-29-2001||08:28 PM||||I seem to have this odd problem with my front passenger side brake assembly.<P>When I am driving and apply the brakes, it makes this real bad creaking sound.  It usually only happens at real low speeds when I am coming to a stop.  I can also feel the creaking in the pedal.<P>Another thing is, It doesn't make the sound if I am braking while I am in reverse. <P>Now I have taken the caliper off and I took out the two outside pistons (One was kinda sticking) and cleaned them up and re-greased them.  But this didn't solve the problem.<P>This happened to me before, but it was when my pads and rotors were totally shot, but they are basically new now (~8000 miles)<P>Any input would be greatly appreciated.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||sjp777||06-29-2001||08:40 PM||||The things you are refering to as outside pistons are actually sliders. There is only one piston behind the inner pad. I had a piston seize last year and it did make noise for a while before it did.<P>Check the pad wear on both wheels. If the pads on the side making noise are thinnerr than on the side not making noise, the one making the noise is probably hanging and will eventually seize.<P>If that's the case, rebuild it or replace it.<P>88 calipers are almost always a little creaky under hard braking, but if it's more than a little, you probably have a problem.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg"> <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||06-29-2001||08:54 PM||||Tell us you used a silicone or synthetic brake grease on them.... petrol bases are not good for brake parts. If you used an oil based product, replace the rubber parts and use the correct grease. (You can't just clean them. the oil permanently swells them.)<P>Oscilation you are describing can be a sign that the rotors were not done right, that's if they were turned... If they weren't turned they could be your problem still...<P>also check where the pads contact the caliper. Those area proably need a slight film of brake grease to. make sure none gets on the pad's friction material or rotor.||||reg||1
Z||000003||AkursedX||06-29-2001||09:30 PM||||Yes of course I used synthetic brake grease on the sliders.    <P>But the rotors have not been turned, they are basically brand new still.  <P>I think my next move will be to take the pads off and inspect the piston. Hopefully my problem lies there...<P>If not, then it's time to go to my dad, lol, he's a mechanic of 25 years.  So it's not like I don't have any experience to back me up.  It's just that he's backloaded on jobs right now, so I want to take care of this problem myself (And with help from the rest of the forum of course!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Galen||06-30-2001||12:10 AM||||When ya find the exact problem. Tell me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hehe<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/kid.gif">         <BR>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe  <BR>AIM: GalenRPG||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||06-30-2001||12:16 AM||||Hey if you are talking about a rather noticable creaking / groaning sound when you are just slipping the brakes at 1mph or less - I think that is normal - i have seen that on lots of differnt vehicles.<P>  sometimes when Im bored at a red light I will let the brakes slip like that - its a weird sound and can be annoying to your passengers - easy to do if you have an auto and you let the brakes off till the car just starts to move.<P>  If this is what you are talking about I dont think there is anything wrong with your brakes.<P>edit: if I were to take a guess at what causes it, ill bet when you slip the brakes at a very low speed the little bit of grabbing that happens causes some part of the car to resonate - like the front springs or some other part of the front end, so when you slip them just right, you get this rather loud moaning sound.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||AkursedX||06-30-2001||12:39 AM||||I know what type of sound you are trying to describe.  But that's not the type of craking I am hearing.  They are totally different types of sounds.  Plus, I can do it when I am completely stopped or at 30mph, it doesn't really matter.  <P>But hopefully I will fix it tomorrow.  It is driving me nuts!!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Coop88||06-30-2001||07:53 AM||||I've tried a couple of different greases on my calipers. None of them have helped. I've tried anti-seize, valvoline high temp grease, and some napa sil-glide grease. I also tried putting some anti-squeak stuff(blue)on the pads where they contact the piston. It worked-for about a day or two. I tried greasing the pins the pads+springs slide on. No luck. My brakes creak to this day. Annoying isn't it? If you find the cure, let us all know. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Coop88||07-01-2001||08:59 AM||||Just pulled the driver's front caliper off my formula. I found that the slide bolts stuck a little bit, but after they were unstuck they move freely. This is after about 2 weeks of using the napa grease. I also found rust between the pad and the piston. I'm gonna take a wire brush to it and grease it. I have a feeling it's the rust causing the creak. Please let us know how your brakes are coming.||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||07-01-2001||12:26 PM||||Just a reminder here, USE ONLY Silicone grease on brake slider pins!<P>It's clear-white, and if it doesn't cost ~$20 for a little bottle, it is the WRONG stuff!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR>That said, slider brakes are IMHO a pain, uugh.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000010||Fformula88||07-01-2001||12:40 PM||||I have had the creaking sound from my front brakes since I bought the car.  I haven't taken them apart to investigate, but the brake pads have lasted 15K miles for me so far, are still in very good condition, and there is no noticeable pulling to indicate a frozen piston.<P>This is my theory... of course its only a guess so don't take it as gospel, but I think the creaking comes from the two spring pins that hold the pads in.  They stay stationary in the caliper while the pads must move along them when the brakes are applied and released.  I'm not certain of course, but it seems like a possibility.<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierosig2.JPG"> <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000011||Coop88||07-01-2001||02:49 PM||||Does anyone have a name of a manufacturer for this silicone grease? I've looked and cannot find it. It would be alot easier if I had the name of the grease, or a store where I could find it. I've looked in autopart stores around here and the people working there just look at me like I just spoke in chinese. Any help would be appreciated. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||AkursedX||07-01-2001||10:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fformula88:<BR><B><P>This is my theory... of course its only a guess so don't take it as gospel, but I think the creaking comes from the two spring pins that hold the pads in.  They stay stationary in the caliper while the pads must move along them when the brakes are applied and released</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I have been thinking of this as being a possibility as well.....<P>Anyways, I am taking the assembly apart tomorrow and I'll see If I can remedy the problem.  <P>I'll post the results as soon as I'm done  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mickey_Moose||07-02-2001||11:18 AM||||have had the same problem with my car for the last couple of years. have checked the entire brake system out (new pads, turned rotors, rebuilt calipers) and it still lives...but now I am thinking that it might actually be the wheel bearings. The passenger one is rough when turning by hand, and am wondering if the sound is some how amplified though the rotor, etc when the pad are pressed into it? I have yet to change the bearings as I can not buy them up here in Canada (no one stocks them) and Autozone.com will not ship to Canada  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>||||reg||9
Z||000014||AkursedX||07-04-2001||12:28 AM||||Ok, well I finally got around to taking apart that brake assembly.  I took and cleaned every part I could and then put it all back together with fresh grease, and it still squeaks!!!  But not as bad.  <P>I will probably go for a caliper rebuild next, and if that is not the problem, I think I know what it is...  When I took out those pins that hold the brake pads in place, I cleaned them on a wire-wheel, and under close inspection, I found a number of notches where the pads slide across them.  I think this is where the sound is coming from.  I wish I had some extra pins around to see if this was true.  <P>I WILL find the cure to this problem!!  ||||reg||1
